#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/bench-campfire — Rails vs the emitted tree, on campfire.
#
# The blog benchmark (scripts/bench) measures a fixture this repo owns;
# the lobsters one (scripts/bench-lobsters) measures a frozen in-process
# visit sequence. This one measures a REAL app over HTTP against REAL
# Rails, both lanes reading the same sqlite file and both signed in as
# the same user — the shape a published claim about "Rails vs
# roundhouse" has to have.
#
# Usage:  scripts/bench-campfire [options] [LANE ...]
#
#   LANE ∈ { rails | rails-nocache | ruby | spinel |
#            rails-1p | rails-nocache-1p | ruby-1p | spinel-1p }
#          (default: rails rails-nocache ruby spinel
#                     rails-1p rails-nocache-1p ruby-1p spinel-1p)
#
#   THE DEPLOYED LANES — what each project would actually run on this box:
#
#     rails          once-campfire AS ITS OWN DOCKERFILE RUNS IT: Thruster
#                    in front of Puma, `(processor_count * 0.666).ceil`
#                    workers x 5 threads from its own config/puma.rb,
#                    jemalloc preloaded, resque-pool alongside, Redis
#                    fragment cache ON, production, YJIT
#     rails-nocache  the same shape with `perform_caching = false`: Rails
#                    actually RENDERING
#     ruby           roundhouse emit on CRuby, the same Puma shape and
#                    the same allocator
#     spinel         the roundhouse spinel BINARY, workers autodetected
#
#   THE ONE-PROCESS LANES — the diagnostic that explains the above:
#
#     rails-1p       Rails, one Puma worker x 5 threads, no proxy, no jobs
#     rails-nocache-1p  the same, rendering rather than reading Redis —
#                    the operand that makes the one-process set a
#                    RENDERER comparison, which is the only thing it can
#                    honestly be
#     ruby-1p        the CRuby emit, same
#     spinel-1p      the binary, SPINEL_WORKERS=1
#
# WHY BOTH SETS. The question this page answers is campfire's own — "can
# it beat the current deployment" — and a deployment is a worker count,
# an allocator, a proxy and a job runner, not a renderer. But a single
# deployed number cannot say WHY it landed where it did: Puma's five
# threads share a GVL where the binary's workers do not, so the
# one-process rows are what separate "their runtime is slower" from "we
# gave them fewer cores". Publish the pair.
#
# NO no-JIT LANES. `rails-int` and `ruby-int` measured within a percent of
# their YJIT siblings on these two pages (40 vs 40 req/s, 22 vs 23) and
# cost a seventh of the run to say nothing.
#
# TWO RAILS LANES, AND THE REASON IS THE WHOLE BENCHMARK. campfire
# renders its message list as `render partial: …, collection: @messages,
# cached: true`. With Redis up, the 40 message fragments are read back
# rather than rendered — `/rooms/1/messages` measures 503 ms cold and
# 5 ms warm. The emitted tree has no fragment cache and renders all 40
# every request, so the warm number is not its opponent: it is Redis's
# throughput. `rails-nocache` is the operand that compares renderers,
# and `rails` is the operand that says what a deployment costs. Publish
# both or publish neither.
#
# Options:
#   --workers N       Puma workers per lane (default 1). PIN IT: campfire's
#                     config/puma.rb sizes itself to the box otherwise, and
#                     the emit's Puma would not.
#   --threads N       threads per worker (default 5)
#   --duration SEC    measurement window (default 20)
#   --warmup SEC      warmup before each measurement (default 20)
#   --connections N   wrk concurrency (default 16 — see the note below)
#   --wrk-threads N   wrk client threads (default 2)
#   --runs N          repeated runs per cell (default 3)
#   --endpoint PATH   URL to bench, repeatable. Default: /rooms/1 and
#                     /rooms/1/messages, the two pages the app exists for.
#   --port N          base port (default 4300; lanes get N, N+1, …)
#   --out DIR         results dir (default bench/results/campfire-<stamp>)
#   --users N / --rooms N / --messages N   seed size, passed to
#                     `campfire-oracle prepare` (default 50 / 5 / 100)
#   --no-sockets      skip the WebSocket phase (HTTP + cold start only)
#   --socket-sizes S  socket counts for that phase (default "1000")
#   --socket-cpus C   cores to pin each socket-phase server to (default 0-3,
#                     once.com/campfire's 1,000-user tier)
#   --socket-db PATH  seeded database for the socket phase (default: the
#                     oracle's). A 1,000-user seed makes every socket a
#                     different user; the default 50-user one reuses them.
#   --socket-cookies PATH   pre-minted cookie file for it
#   --skip-prepare    reuse the oracle tree and emit already on disk
#   --keep            leave servers running on exit
#
# WHY 16 CONNECTIONS AND NOT 64. A campfire room page is ~400 KB and
# takes single-digit milliseconds to build; at 64 connections against one
# worker the queue depth, not the app, sets the latency, and both lanes
# converge on the same number. 16 keeps the server the bottleneck.
#
# EVERY REQUEST IS AUTHENTICATED. campfire has no anonymous pages worth
# measuring — an unsigned-in client gets a 302 to /session/new, which is
# a benchmark of a redirect. Each lane signs in through its own real form
# (the two trees do not share a cookie secret) and wrk carries that
# lane's `Cookie:` header.
#
# BOTH LANES READ THE SAME FILE. `prepare` seeds once through Rails and
# copies the result into the emit's storage/, so a row-count difference
# can never explain a throughput difference.
#
# Output:
#   <out>/per-run.json    raw records, one JSON object per line
#   <out>/summary.json    median per (lane, endpoint)
#   <out>/summary.md      rendered table
#   <out>/parity.txt      tag-tally diff per endpoint, both lanes
#   <out>/coldstart.json  one record per boot: to an answer, to a page
#   <out>/sockets.json    the WebSocket tier, both lanes, folded from
#                         scripts/campfire-cable-sweep
#   <out>/env.json        provenance: campfire SHA, Rails revision, rubies,
#                         and what each lane ACTUALLY ran
#
# Every one of those is a section on the published page, and
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` fetches them BY NAME from the bench box — a
# new sidecar that is not added to that list is a section that silently
# does not appear.
#
# Requires: wrk, ruby, bundler, sqlite3, curl, cargo.

set -euo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
cd "$REPO_ROOT"

ORIG_ARGS=("$@")

LANES=()
WORKERS=1
THREADS=5
DURATION=20
WARMUP=20
CONNECTIONS=16
WRK_THREADS=2
RUNS=3
ENDPOINTS=()
BASE_PORT=4300
OUT_DIR=""
USERS=50
ROOMS=5
MESSAGES=100
SKIP_PREPARE=0
KEEP=0
SOCKETS=1
SOCKET_SIZES="1000"
SOCKET_CPUS="0-3"
SOCKET_DB=""
SOCKET_COOKIES=""
APP="$HOME/git/once-campfire"

while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
        --workers)     WORKERS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --threads)     THREADS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --duration)    DURATION="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --warmup)      WARMUP="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --connections) CONNECTIONS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --wrk-threads) WRK_THREADS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --runs)        RUNS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --endpoint)    ENDPOINTS+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
        --port)        BASE_PORT="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --out)         OUT_DIR="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --users)       USERS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --rooms)       ROOMS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --messages)    MESSAGES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --app)         APP="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --sockets)     SOCKETS=1; shift ;;
        --no-sockets)  SOCKETS=0; shift ;;
        --socket-sizes) SOCKET_SIZES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-cpus)  SOCKET_CPUS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-db)    SOCKET_DB="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-cookies) SOCKET_COOKIES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --skip-prepare) SKIP_PREPARE=1; shift ;;
        --keep)        KEEP=1; shift ;;
        -h|--help)     sed -n '2,107p' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
        rails|rails-nocache|ruby|spinel|rails-1p|rails-nocache-1p|ruby-1p|spinel-1p) LANES+=("$1"); shift ;;
        *) echo "bench-campfire: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

(( ${#LANES[@]} )) || LANES=(rails rails-nocache ruby spinel rails-1p rails-nocache-1p ruby-1p spinel-1p)
(( ${#ENDPOINTS[@]} )) || ENDPOINTS=(/rooms/1 /rooms/1/messages)
[[ -n "$OUT_DIR" ]] || OUT_DIR="$REPO_ROOT/bench/results/campfire-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"

ORACLE="$REPO_ROOT/build/campfire-oracle"
EMIT="$REPO_ROOT/build/transpiled-campfire-ruby"
SP_EMIT="$REPO_ROOT/build/transpiled-campfire-spinel"
# The binary verifies the session cookie it minted itself, so any stable
# value works — but it has to be the SAME one the cable sweep uses, or a
# pre-minted cookie file cannot be shared between the two harnesses.
SPINEL_SECRET="${SECRET_KEY_BASE:-campfire-oracle-benchmark-secret-not-a-deployment}"
# ABSOLUTE, IMMEDIATELY. Every path derived from here is handed to a
# script that `cd`s — the oracle into its own tree, the cable sweep into
# the server's — and a relative one then resolves against THAT directory.
# The first published run passed `--out bench/results/campfire/<stamp>`
# and lost both deployed Rails lanes and the entire WebSocket phase to
# it, each failing as "No such file or directory" on a log it was trying
# to write. Every validation run before it had used an absolute /tmp
# path, so nothing caught it.
mkdir -p "$OUT_DIR"
OUT_DIR="$(cd "$OUT_DIR" && pwd)"
LOG_DIR="$OUT_DIR/logs"
mkdir -p "$LOG_DIR"

# A lane the harness asked for and could not measure. bench-report draws
# a banner from this file, which is the difference between a page that is
# missing a lane and a page that SAYS it is missing a lane and why — the
# whole point of publishing status rather than results.
SKIPPED="$OUT_DIR/skipped.json"
: > "$SKIPPED"
skip_lane() {   # $1 = lane, $2 = stage, $3 = reason, $4 = log to tail
    local tail_txt=""
    [[ -n "${4:-}" && -f "${4:-}" ]] && tail_txt="$(tail -3 "$4" | tr -d '\r' | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/"/\\"/g' | cut -c1-300)"
    printf '{"target":"%s","stage":"%s","reason":"%s","log_tail":"%s"}\n' \
        "$1" "$2" "$3" "$tail_txt" >> "$SKIPPED"
}


step() { printf '\033[36m→\033[0m %s\n' "$*"; }
ok()   { printf '\033[32m✓\033[0m %s\n' "$*"; }
fail() { printf '\033[31m✗\033[0m %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
die()  { fail "$*"; exit 1; }

command -v wrk >/dev/null || die "wrk not on PATH"

SERVER_PID=""
cleanup() {
    local code=$?
    if (( KEEP == 0 )) && [[ -n "$SERVER_PID" ]]; then
        pkill -P "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        kill -TERM "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        sleep 0.3
        kill -KILL "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
    exit "$code"
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

# Sum PSS across a PID and its descendants (KB), or nothing where the
# kernel does not offer it (macOS, older Linux).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS BESIDE THE RSS SUM. Adding up RSS over a process tree
# counts every shared page once per process. A clustered Puma with eight
# workers therefore reads ~3.2 GB where its real footprint is far lower,
# while a single-process binary has nothing to double-count — so the
# error runs entirely in OUR favour, on the biggest memory number the
# page prints. PSS divides each shared page by the number of processes
# sharing it, which is the number a deployer's budget actually sees, and
# it is what the WebSocket phase and the runtime ledger have always used.
sum_pss_kb() {
    local pids="$1" stack="$1"
    [[ -r "/proc/$1/smaps_rollup" ]] || { echo ""; return 0; }
    while [[ -n "$stack" ]]; do
        local next=""
        for p in $stack; do
            for k in $(pgrep -P "$p" 2>/dev/null || true); do
                pids="$pids $k"; next="$next $k"
            done
        done
        stack="$next"
    done
    local total=0 v
    for p in $pids; do
        v="$(awk '/^Pss:/ {s += $2} END {print s+0}' "/proc/$p/smaps_rollup" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
        total=$(( total + ${v:-0} ))
    done
    echo "$total"
}

# Sum RSS across a PID and its descendants (KB). Puma's master carries
# almost nothing; the workers are the measurement.
sum_rss_kb() {
    local pids="$1" stack="$1"
    while [[ -n "$stack" ]]; do
        local next=""
        for p in $stack; do
            for k in $(pgrep -P "$p" 2>/dev/null || true); do
                pids="$pids $k"; next="$next $k"
            done
        done
        stack="$next"
    done
    local total=0 r
    for p in $pids; do
        r="$(ps -o rss= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ' || echo 0)"
        total=$(( total + ${r:-0} ))
    done
    echo "$total"
}

# Their Dockerfile preloads jemalloc; every Ruby lane here does too, so
# the memory column compares runtimes and not malloc implementations.
# Echoes a path or nothing — a lane that cannot find one records ABSENT
# rather than serving a shape it does not admit to.
find_jemalloc() {
    local c
    for c in /usr/local/lib/libjemalloc.so \
             /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /usr/lib64/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /opt/homebrew/lib/libjemalloc.dylib \
             /usr/local/opt/jemalloc/lib/libjemalloc.dylib; do
        [[ -e "$c" ]] && { echo "$c"; return 0; }
    done
    c="$(ldconfig -p 2>/dev/null | awk '/libjemalloc\.so\.2/ {print $NF; exit}')"
    [[ -n "$c" && -e "$c" ]] && { echo "$c"; return 0; }
    return 1
}

# campfire's own worker count for THIS box: `(Concurrent.processor_count
# * 0.666).ceil` out of its config/puma.rb. The emit's Puma has no such
# formula, so the ruby lane is given the number rather than guessing at
# one — the two lanes must be the same shape or the comparison is a core
# count again.
deployed_worker_count() {
    local n
    n="$(getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || echo 1)"
    ruby -e 'print (ARGV[0].to_i * 0.666).ceil' "$n"
}

wait_for_port() {
    local url="$1" budget="${2:-60}"
    local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + budget ))
    local code
    while (( $(date +%s) < deadline )); do
        # An answer from the APP means it is up: campfire 302s an
        # unsigned-in client, so `curl --fail` would reject a perfectly
        # healthy boot. But 502 is Thruster answering for a Puma that has
        # not finished booting — accepting it declared the deployed lane
        # ready four seconds early and would have handed the cold-start
        # measurement the proxy's startup instead of Rails'.
        # `|| true`, and it is not decoration. Under `set -e` a failed
        # command substitution exits the harness, and curl exits 7 for
        # "connection refused" — the NORMAL answer while a server is
        # still booting, i.e. every time this loop is doing its job.
        #
        # NOT `|| echo 000`: `-w '%{http_code}'` already prints 000 on a
        # failed connection, so the fallback appended a SECOND one and
        # the guard below compared "000\n000" against "000", found them
        # different, and declared every lane up before it had started.
        code="$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$url" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        [[ "$code" != "000" && "$code" != "502" && "$code" != "503" ]] && return 0
        sleep 0.3
    done
    return 1
}

# ── prepare ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

if (( SKIP_PREPARE == 0 )); then
    step "preparing the Rails oracle ($USERS users, $ROOMS rooms, $MESSAGES msgs/room)"
    scripts/campfire-oracle prepare --app "$APP" --out "$ORACLE" \
        --users "$USERS" --rooms "$ROOMS" --messages "$MESSAGES" >/dev/null \
        || die "campfire-oracle prepare failed"

    step "transpiling campfire → $EMIT"
    cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target ruby "$APP" \
        -o "$EMIT" --allow-unsupported > "$LOG_DIR/emit.log" 2>&1 \
        || die "transpile failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit.log)"

    step "bundling the emit"
    (cd "$EMIT" && bundle install >/dev/null 2>&1) || die "emit bundle install failed"

    step "building the emit's assets"
    (cd "$EMIT" && rake assets >/dev/null 2>&1) || step "  (no assets task — continuing)"
fi

# ── the spinel binary ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Campfire compiles, builds and serves on spinel now. Until 2026-09-04
# this harness emitted the tree for the ARCHIVE only, with a comment
# saying it "does not compile yet" — true when written, and the reason
# the published page had no binary lane while the binary was holding five
# thousand WebSocket connections in another harness.
#
# Strict emit (no --allow-unsupported), because CI holds campfire's
# strict emit at zero errors and a regression should be loud here.
build_spinel_binary() {
    step "transpiling campfire → spinel (strict)"
    cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target spinel "$APP" \
        -o "$SP_EMIT" > "$LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log" 2>&1 \
        || { fail "strict spinel emit failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log)"; return 1; }
    command -v spin >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || { fail "spin not on PATH — cannot build the spinel binary"; return 1; }
    step "building the spinel binary (a few minutes)"
    ( cd "$SP_EMIT" && make build ) > "$LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log" 2>&1 \
        || { fail "spinel build failed (see $LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log)"; return 1; }
    [[ -x "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" ]] \
        || { fail "spinel build produced no binary"; return 1; }
    ok "spinel binary at $SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog"
}

WANT_SPINEL=0
for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do [[ "$_l" == spinel* ]] && WANT_SPINEL=1; done
if (( WANT_SPINEL )); then
    if ! build_spinel_binary; then
        fail "dropping the spinel lanes"
        NEW_LANES=()
        for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do
            if [[ "$_l" == spinel* ]]; then
                skip_lane "$_l" "build" "the spinel binary could not be built" "$LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log"
            else
                NEW_LANES+=("$_l")
            fi
        done
        LANES=("${NEW_LANES[@]}")
    fi
fi

# THE SAME ROWS, ALWAYS. Copied on every run, not only on prepare: a
# previous run's POSTs would otherwise leave the two databases apart, and
# a benchmark whose lanes read different data is not one.
step "staging the seeded DB into the emit"
[[ -f "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3" ]] \
    || die "no seeded production DB at $ORACLE — run without --skip-prepare"
mkdir -p "$EMIT/storage"
cp "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3" "$EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3"
# The binary reads the same rows from its own tree. WAL and SHM go with
# it: a stale -wal beside a freshly copied database is a different
# database, and sqlite will happily read the pair.
if [[ -d "$SP_EMIT" ]]; then
    mkdir -p "$SP_EMIT/storage"
    cp "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3" "$SP_EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3"
    rm -f "$SP_EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3-wal" "$SP_EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3-shm"
fi
rm -f "$EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3-wal" "$EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3-shm"

# ── per-lane lifecycle ───────────────────────────────────────────────

start_lane() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2"
    local log="$LOG_DIR/$lane.log"
    local shape="$LOG_DIR/shape-$lane.txt"
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-nocache)
            # AS THEIR DOCKERFILE RUNS IT. `--deployed` is the whole
            # difference: campfire's own worker formula instead of a
            # pinned count, jemalloc, Thruster, resque-pool.
            local nocache=""; [[ "$lane" == "rails-nocache" ]] && nocache="--no-fragment-cache"
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --deployed --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" \
                --threads "$THREADS" --shape-report "$shape" \
                $nocache > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        rails-1p|rails-nocache-1p)
            local nc1=""; [[ "$lane" == "rails-nocache-1p" ]] && nc1="--no-fragment-cache"
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" \
                --workers 1 --threads "$THREADS" --shape-report "$shape" \
                $nc1 > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        ruby|ruby-1p)
            # RAILS_ENV=production, for the same reason the oracle serves it
            # (see campfire-oracle's header: development measured 11.4 req/s
            # against production's 141.1 — the SAME app, 12x apart). The emit
            # reads `Rails.env` too, and with it unset every ruby lane booted
            # "development" while the lane it is compared against booted
            # production. On campfire's benchmarked routes that costs nothing
            # — the only env branch in the emitted tree is in
            # `users/profiles/show` — but an asymmetry that happens to be
            # free today is not one to leave in a published comparison. The
            # database does not move with it: the emit resolves its path from
            # DB_PATH or the literal `storage/development.sqlite3`.
            #
            # THE SAME ALLOCATOR AS THEIRS. Their Dockerfile preloads
            # jemalloc; this lane is Ruby too, and a memory column that
            # compared their allocator against ours would be measuring
            # malloc rather than runtimes.
            local jem; jem="$(find_jemalloc || true)"
            local w=1
            [[ "$lane" == "ruby" ]] && w="$(deployed_worker_count)"
            echo "jemalloc=${jem:-ABSENT}" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=$w" >> "$shape"
            ( cd "$EMIT" && exec env RAILS_ENV=production RUBY_YJIT_ENABLE=1 \
                RAILS_MAX_THREADS="$THREADS" ${jem:+LD_PRELOAD="$jem"} \
                bundle exec puma -p "$port" -w "$w" -t "$THREADS:$THREADS" \
                config.ru ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        spinel|spinel-1p)
            # THE BINARY. One process either way — it has no cluster mode
            # — so the lane pair is its OS worker count: autodetected for
            # the deployed row, one for the diagnostic. No allocator knob:
            # it does not use Ruby's.
            local workers_env=()
            if [[ "$lane" == "spinel-1p" ]]; then
                workers_env=(SPINEL_WORKERS=1); echo "workers=1" > "$shape"
            else
                echo "workers=autodetected" > "$shape"
            fi
            ( cd "$SP_EMIT" && exec env "${workers_env[@]}" \
                DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=64 BLOG_DB=storage/development.sqlite3 \
                PORT="$port" SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" \
                ./build/bin/blog ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
    esac
    wait_for_port "http://127.0.0.1:$port/" || {
        fail "$lane did not come up within 60s — tail of $log:"
        tail -20 "$log" >&2
        return 1
    }
}

# Sign in through the lane's OWN form and echo the `Cookie:` header
# value. The two trees do not share a cookie secret, so a jar minted
# against one is a 302 against the other; this is not a detail that can
# be shared between lanes.
login_cookie() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2"
    local jar="$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt"
    rm -f "$jar"
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-nocache|rails-1p|rails-nocache-1p)
            scripts/campfire-oracle login --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" --jar "$jar" >/dev/null \
                || { fail "$lane sign-in failed"; return 1; }
            ;;
        ruby|ruby-1p|spinel|spinel-1p)
            # No CSRF token scrape: the emitted tree's session form does
            # not gate on one, and asking for a token it never issues
            # would fail for a reason that has nothing to do with speed.
            local code
            code="$(curl -sS -c "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                    -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session" \
                    --data-urlencode "email_address=user1@example.com" \
                    --data-urlencode "password=secret123456")"
            [[ "$code" == "302" ]] || { fail "$lane sign-in returned $code"; return 1; }
            ;;
    esac
    awk '/session_token|_campfire_session/ {printf "%s=%s; ", $6, $7}' "$jar"
}

parse_wrk() {
    local out="$1" req_sec p50 p99
    req_sec="$(grep -E '^Requests/sec:' "$out" | awk '{print $2}')"
    p50="$(awk '/Latency Distribution/{f=1;next} f && /^[[:space:]]*50%/{print $2; exit}' "$out")"
    p99="$(awk '/Latency Distribution/{f=1;next} f && /^[[:space:]]*99%/{print $2; exit}' "$out")"
    norm() {
        [[ -z "$1" ]] && { echo null; return; }
        awk -v v="$1" 'BEGIN {
            n = v; gsub(/[a-zA-Z]+$/, "", n)
            u = v; sub(/^[0-9.]+/, "", u)
            if (u == "us") printf "%.3f", n/1000
            else if (u == "ms") printf "%.3f", n+0
            else if (u == "s") printf "%.3f", n*1000
            else printf "null"
        }'
    }
    [[ -z "$req_sec" ]] && req_sec=null
    echo "{\"req_sec\":$req_sec,\"p50_ms\":$(norm "$p50"),\"p99_ms\":$(norm "$p99")}"
}

# ── run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PER_RUN="$OUT_DIR/per-run.json"
COLD="$OUT_DIR/coldstart.json"
: > "$PER_RUN"
: > "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt"
: > "$COLD"

# ── cold start ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The first measure on campfire's own list, and the one no page here has
# ever carried. TWO numbers, because for a chat app they are different
# questions:
#
#   accept_ms  exec -> the port answers at all. For the deployed Rails
#              lane this is Thruster, its Puma master, and eight forked
#              workers; for the binary it is one execve.
#   page_ms    exec -> a signed-in GET of /rooms/1 comes back 200. This
#              is the honest one: it includes the first render, which on
#              a fragment-cached lane is the COLD cache (measured at
#              503 ms against a 5 ms warm read), and on ours is the only
#              render there is.
#
# The cookie is minted against a server that is then stopped, so the jar
# outlives the boot it was made in — every lane signs itself in once,
# before this, and reuses that jar here.
cold_start() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2" jar="$3" reps="${4:-3}"
    local rep t0 t_accept t_page code
    for rep in $(seq 1 "$reps"); do
        t0="$(date +%s.%N)"
        if ! start_lane_quiet "$lane" "$port"; then
            fail "$lane cold-start boot $rep failed"
            stop_lane
            return 0
        fi
        t_accept="$(date +%s.%N)"
        # The room page, authenticated, until it is really a 200: a lane
        # still booting answers 502, and a jar that has gone stale
        # answers 302, which would time out here rather than silently
        # recording a redirect as a rendered page.
        local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + 120 ))
        while (( $(date +%s) < deadline )); do
            code="$(curl -s -b "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                    "http://127.0.0.1:$port/rooms/1" 2>/dev/null || true)"
            [[ "$code" == "200" ]] && break
            sleep 0.05
        done
        t_page="$(date +%s.%N)"
        stop_lane
        printf '{"target":"%s","rep":%d,"accept_ms":%s,"page_ms":%s,"page_code":"%s"}\n' \
            "$lane" "$rep" \
            "$(awk -v a="$t0" -v b="$t_accept" 'BEGIN{printf "%.1f", (b-a)*1000}')" \
            "$(awk -v a="$t0" -v b="$t_page" 'BEGIN{printf "%.1f", (b-a)*1000}')" \
            "$code" >> "$COLD"
    done
}

# start_lane without the banner, for the cold-start reps.
start_lane_quiet() { start_lane "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1; }

stop_lane() {
    [[ -z "$SERVER_PID" ]] && return 0
    kill -TERM "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
    local i
    for i in $(seq 1 100); do kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || break; sleep 0.1; done
    kill -KILL "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
    wait "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
    SERVER_PID=""
}

port="$BASE_PORT"
for lane in "${LANES[@]}"; do
    # Say what THIS lane will actually run. The old banner printed
    # `$WORKERS` for every lane, so a deployed Rails booting eight
    # workers and a binary autodetecting twelve both announced
    # "1 worker(s)".
    case "$lane" in
        rails-nocache-1p)    shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads, RENDERING (no fragment cache)" ;;
        rails|rails-nocache) shape_note="campfire's own worker formula x $THREADS threads, thruster, jemalloc, resque-pool" ;;
        ruby)                shape_note="$(deployed_worker_count) worker(s) x $THREADS threads, jemalloc" ;;
        spinel)              shape_note="one process, workers autodetected" ;;
        spinel-1p)           shape_note="one process, SPINEL_WORKERS=1" ;;
        *)                   shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads" ;;
    esac
    step "start $lane on :$port ($shape_note)"
    if ! start_lane "$lane" "$port"; then
        fail "$lane failed to start; skipping lane"
        skip_lane "$lane" "boot" "did not answer on :$port within 60s" "$LOG_DIR/$lane.log"
        stop_lane
        port=$(( port + 1 )); continue
    fi
    cookie="$(login_cookie "$lane" "$port")" || {
        fail "$lane sign-in failed; skipping lane"
        skip_lane "$lane" "sign-in" "the lane booted but would not sign a user in" "$LOG_DIR/$lane.log"
        stop_lane
        port=$(( port + 1 )); continue
    }
    ok "$lane up and signed in"

    # WARM THE FRAGMENT CACHE for the lanes that have one, deliberately
    # and on the record. The first request to a cached page is 100x the
    # rest (503 ms against 5 ms), and wrk's own warmup would absorb it
    # anyway — doing it here means the `rails` lane measures the steady
    # state it is meant to, rather than one cold render smeared across a
    # short run.
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-1p)
            for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
                curl -s -b "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" -o /dev/null "http://127.0.0.1:$port$ep"
            done
            ;;
    esac

    # Parity capture BEFORE the load: one authenticated GET per endpoint,
    # saved so `parity.txt` can say what each lane actually rendered. A
    # throughput number for a page that is not the same page is not a
    # comparison, and this is the cheapest possible record of that.
    for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
        body="$LOG_DIR/body-$lane-$(echo "$ep" | tr '/' '_').html"
        code="$(curl -s -b "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" -o "$body" -w '%{http_code}' \
                "http://127.0.0.1:$port$ep")"
        tags="$(ruby -e 'print File.read(ARGV[0]).scan(/<([a-zA-Z][-a-zA-Z0-9]*)/).size' "$body")"
        printf '%s\t%s\tstatus=%s\ttags=%s\tbytes=%s\n' \
            "$lane" "$ep" "$code" "$tags" "$(wc -c < "$body" | tr -d ' ')" \
            >> "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt"
    done

    for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
        url="http://127.0.0.1:$port$ep"
        for run in $(seq 1 "$RUNS"); do
            step "measure $lane $ep run=$run/$RUNS"
            wrk -t"$WRK_THREADS" -c"$CONNECTIONS" -d"${WARMUP}s" \
                -H "Cookie: $cookie" "$url" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
            wrk_out="$LOG_DIR/wrk-$lane-$(echo "$ep" | tr '/' '_')-$run.txt"
            rss_out="$LOG_DIR/rss-$lane-$(echo "$ep" | tr '/' '_')-$run.txt"
            : > "$rss_out"
            # Two columns per sample: RSS sum, then PSS sum (empty
            # where the kernel has no smaps_rollup).
            ( while kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null; do
                  echo "$(sum_rss_kb "$SERVER_PID") $(sum_pss_kb "$SERVER_PID")" >> "$rss_out"
                  sleep 1
              done ) &
            rss_pid=$!
            wrk -t"$WRK_THREADS" -c"$CONNECTIONS" -d"${DURATION}s" --latency \
                -H "Cookie: $cookie" "$url" > "$wrk_out" 2>&1 || true
            kill "$rss_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
            wait "$rss_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
            rss="$(awk '{print $1}' "$rss_out" | sort -n | awk '{a[NR]=$1} END {print NR ? a[int((NR+1)/2)] : 0}')"
            pss="$(awk 'NF>1 {print $2}' "$rss_out" | sort -n | awk '{a[NR]=$1} END {print NR ? a[int((NR+1)/2)] : ""}')"
            printf '{"target":"%s","workers":%d,"endpoint":"%s","run":%d,"rss_kb":%s,"pss_kb":%s,%s}\n' \
                "$lane" "$WORKERS" "$ep" "$run" "${rss:-0}" "${pss:-null}" \
                "$(parse_wrk "$wrk_out" | sed 's/^{//;s/}$//')" >> "$PER_RUN"
        done
    done

    stop_lane

    # Cold start, after the load and with this lane's jar in hand. Redis
    # is NOT flushed between reps, deliberately: restarting a campfire
    # container does not empty the cache its own Procfile keeps in a
    # separate process, so a warm Redis is what their restart actually
    # sees. Rep 1 still pays a cold render for anything the load did not
    # touch.
    step "cold start $lane (3 boots)"
    cold_start "$lane" "$port" "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" 3

    port=$(( port + 1 ))
done

# ── the WebSocket phase ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The measure campfire's own discussion calls the one that matters most —
# "how many concurrent WebSocket connections a given box will hold" — and
# the one this page has never carried, because it lives in a different
# harness. It still does: `scripts/campfire-cable-sweep` drives both
# lanes with one client, and duplicating any of it here would be a second
# implementation of the thing that makes the comparison honest. This runs
# it and folds its results in.
#
# PINNED TO FOUR CORES, which is once.com/campfire's own 1,000-user tier
# (4 CPU / 8 GB). Unpinned it would measure this box instead of a tier,
# and campfire's puma.rb would size its worker count to the whole
# machine while the row claimed to be a tier.
run_socket_phase() {
    local sweep="$REPO_ROOT/scripts/campfire-cable-sweep"
    [[ -x "$sweep" ]] || { fail "no campfire-cable-sweep — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }

    local db="${SOCKET_DB:-$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3}"
    local cookies="${SOCKET_COOKIES:-$ORACLE/tmp/session-cookies.txt}"
    if [[ ! -f "$cookies" ]]; then
        step "minting $USERS session cookies for the socket phase"
        scripts/campfire-oracle cookies --out "$ORACLE" --count "$USERS" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || { fail "could not mint cookies — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }
    fi
    [[ -f "$cookies" ]] || { fail "no cookie file at $cookies — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }

    # Rooms come from the seed, not from a default: the sweep spreads N
    # sockets over ceil(N / per-room) rooms and a room the seed does not
    # have answers 302, which reads as a silent zero delivery.
    local biggest=0 n
    for n in $SOCKET_SIZES; do (( n > biggest )) && biggest=$n; done
    local per_room=$(( (biggest + ROOMS - 1) / ROOMS ))
    (( per_room < 1 )) && per_room=1

    # campfire's own formula at the tier's core count, which is what its
    # puma.rb would compute inside a 4-CPU container.
    local tier_cpus rails_workers
    tier_cpus="$(awk -F- '{ if (NF==2) print $2-$1+1; else print 1 }' <<< "$SOCKET_CPUS")"
    rails_workers="$(ruby -e 'print (ARGV[0].to_i * 0.666).ceil' "$tier_cpus")"

    if [[ -x "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" ]]; then
        step "socket phase: the binary, $SOCKET_SIZES sockets on cpus $SOCKET_CPUS"
        "$sweep" --lane binary --bin "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" \
            --db "$db" --cookies "$cookies" --cpus "$SOCKET_CPUS" \
            --workers "$tier_cpus" --sizes "$SOCKET_SIZES" --per-room "$per_room" \
            --secret "$SPINEL_SECRET" --out "$OUT_DIR/sockets-binary" \
            > "$LOG_DIR/sockets-binary.log" 2>&1 \
            || fail "the binary's socket lane failed (see $LOG_DIR/sockets-binary.log)"
    else
        fail "no spinel binary — the socket phase has no binary lane"
    fi

    step "socket phase: rails, $SOCKET_SIZES sockets on cpus $SOCKET_CPUS ($rails_workers workers)"
    "$sweep" --lane rails --oracle "$ORACLE" \
        --db "$db" --cookies "$cookies" --cpus "$SOCKET_CPUS" \
        --workers "$rails_workers" --threads "$THREADS" \
        --sizes "$SOCKET_SIZES" --per-room "$per_room" \
        --out "$OUT_DIR/sockets-rails" \
        > "$LOG_DIR/sockets-rails.log" 2>&1 \
        || fail "the rails socket lane failed (see $LOG_DIR/sockets-rails.log)"

    # One file for the renderer, lanes labelled the way the HTTP rows are.
    ruby - "$OUT_DIR/sockets-binary/results.jsonl" "$OUT_DIR/sockets-rails/results.jsonl" \
           "$OUT_DIR/sockets.json" <<'RUBY'
require "json"
binary, rails, out = ARGV
rows = []
{ "spinel" => binary, "rails" => rails }.each do |lane, path|
  next unless File.exist?(path)
  File.readlines(path, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?).each do |line|
    d = JSON.parse(line) rescue next
    rows << {
      "target" => lane, "sockets" => d["sockets"],
      "subscribers_per_room" => d["subscribers_per_room"],
      "storm_s" => d.dig("storm", "seconds"), "storm_cpu_s" => d.dig("storm", "cpu_seconds"),
      "failed" => d.dig("storm", "failed"),
      "idle_cores" => d.dig("idle", "cpu_cores"),
      "rss_mb" => d.dig("idle", "rss_mb"), "pss_mb" => d.dig("idle", "pss_mb"),
      "pings" => d.dig("idle", "pings"),
      "delivered" => d.dig("chat", "delivered"), "expected" => d.dig("chat", "expected"),
      "frame_p50_ms" => d.dig("chat", "frame_p50_ms"), "frame_p99_ms" => d.dig("chat", "frame_p99_ms"),
      "chat_cores" => d.dig("chat", "cpu_cores"),
      "driver_max_pass_ms" => d.dig("chat", "driver_max_pass_ms"),
      "teardown_cpu_s" => d.dig("teardown", "cpu_seconds"),
      "server" => d["server"],
    }
  end
end
File.write(out, JSON.pretty_generate(rows) + "\n") unless rows.empty?
RUBY
    [[ -f "$OUT_DIR/sockets.json" ]] && ok "socket phase -> $OUT_DIR/sockets.json"
    return 0
}
(( SOCKETS )) && run_socket_phase

# ── provenance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The machine-and-toolchain half is scripts/bench-env — the SAME capture
# the blog bench publishes, so the two pages' appendices are comparable
# and neither is a hand-rolled second copy free to drift. A hand-rolled
# one already cost a whole run's page: it wrote `"host": "<uname>"` where
# bench-report reads a map, and the report aborted on it.
#
# Campfire's own half is merged on top. It is the half a reader needs
# most and the half no generic capture can know: WHICH campfire (the app
# SHA) and WHICH Rails (campfire's Gemfile tracks rails/rails main, so
# the framework is pinned only by the lockfile's git revision), plus the
# seed size and whether the fragment cache was on. campfire-oracle
# writes those into oracle.json at prepare time.
"$REPO_ROOT/scripts/bench-env" \
    --workers     "$WORKERS" \
    --concurrency "$CONNECTIONS" \
    --runs        "$RUNS" \
    --duration    "$DURATION" \
    --warmup      "$WARMUP" \
    --threads     "$THREADS" \
    --wrk-threads "$WRK_THREADS" \
    --endpoints   "${ENDPOINTS[*]}" \
    --targets     "${LANES[*]}" \
    --command     "scripts/bench-campfire ${ORIG_ARGS[*]}" \
    > "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json" 2>/dev/null || echo '{}' > "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json"

ruby - "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json" "$ORACLE/oracle.json" "$OUT_DIR/env.json" \
       "$ORACLE/Gemfile.lock" "$EMIT/Gemfile.lock" <<'RUBY'
require "json"

base_path, oracle_path, out_path, rails_lock, emit_lock = ARGV

def read_json(path)
  JSON.parse(File.read(path))
rescue Errno::ENOENT, JSON::ParserError
  nil
end

env = read_json(base_path) || {}
env["benchmark"] = "campfire"
oracle = read_json(oracle_path)
env["oracle"] = oracle if oracle

# The lane note travels WITH the data. A campfire page that carries
# `rails` without saying what `rails-nocache` is has published the number
# this harness exists to prevent — and the renderer is generic, so the
# only place that sentence can live is here, beside the run it describes.
# The app server is part of the measurement and the two lanes resolve it
# separately — campfire's lockfile pins one, the emitted Gemfile asks for
# whatever is current. They came out 7.2.1 and 8.0.1, which the published
# logs said and the published page did not.
def puma_version(lock_path)
  File.read(lock_path)[/^\s+puma \(([^)]+)\)/, 1]
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  nil
end

servers = {
  "rails lanes" => puma_version(rails_lock)&.then { |v| "puma #{v}" },
  "emit lanes"  => puma_version(emit_lock)&.then { |v| "puma #{v}" },
}.compact
env["servers"] = servers unless servers.empty?

env["notes"] = [
  "<code>rails</code> serves campfire's message fragments from Redis " \
  "(<code>cached: true</code>); <code>rails-nocache</code> renders them. " \
  "The emitted tree has no fragment cache, so <code>rails-nocache</code> " \
  "is the lane that compares renderers and <code>rails</code> is the lane " \
  "that says what a deployment costs. Publish both or publish neither.",
  "The deployed lanes are what each project would run on this box. " \
  "<code>rails</code> is once-campfire AS ITS OWN DOCKERFILE RUNS IT: " \
  "Thruster in front of Puma, <code>(processor_count * 0.666).ceil</code> " \
  "workers x 5 threads from its own <code>config/puma.rb</code>, jemalloc " \
  "preloaded, resque-pool alongside. <code>ruby</code> is the CRuby emit " \
  "in the same Puma shape with the same allocator; <code>spinel</code> is " \
  "the binary with its workers autodetected.",
  "The <code>-1p</code> lanes are the diagnostic: one process each, no " \
  "proxy and no job runner. They exist because a deployed number cannot " \
  "say WHY it landed where it did — Puma's five threads share a GVL where " \
  "the binary's workers do not, so these rows separate \"their runtime is " \
  "slower\" from \"we gave them fewer cores\".",
  "Known omissions, stated rather than discovered: the WebSocket phase's " \
  "rails lane runs Puma with campfire's worker count and its allocator but " \
  "WITHOUT Thruster and resque-pool, and no lane terminates TLS.",
]

# WHAT EACH LANE ACTUALLY GOT. A deployed lane that claimed jemalloc and
# ran without it would be the exact failure this shape is meant to end,
# so the achieved parts travel with the data rather than the intent.
shapes = {}
Dir[File.join(File.dirname(out_path), "logs", "shape-*.txt")].sort.each do |f|
  lane = File.basename(f, ".txt").sub(/\Ashape-/, "")
  parts = File.readlines(f, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?)
  shapes[lane] = parts unless parts.empty?
end
env["lane_shapes"] = shapes unless shapes.empty?

File.write(out_path, JSON.pretty_generate(env) + "\n")
RUBY
rm -f "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json"

# ── summarize ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same shape scripts/bench writes, so bench-report renders either.

ruby - "$PER_RUN" "$OUT_DIR/summary.json" "$OUT_DIR/summary.md" "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt" <<'RUBY'
require "json"
per_run, sj, sm, parity = ARGV
records = File.readlines(per_run, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?).map { |l| JSON.parse(l) }
abort "no measurements" if records.empty?

def median(xs)
  xs = xs.compact.sort
  return nil if xs.empty?
  xs.size.odd? ? xs[xs.size / 2] : (xs[xs.size / 2 - 1] + xs[xs.size / 2]) / 2.0
end

cells = records.group_by { |r| [r["target"], r["workers"], r["endpoint"]] }.map do |(t, w, e), runs|
  rss_kb = median(runs.map { |r| r["rss_kb"] }.reject { |v| v.to_i.zero? })
  pss_kb = median(runs.map { |r| r["pss_kb"] }.compact.reject { |v| v.to_i.zero? })
  req = median(runs.map { |r| r["req_sec"] })
  { target: t, workers: w, endpoint: e, runs: runs.size,
    req_sec: req, p50_ms: median(runs.map { |r| r["p50_ms"] }),
    p99_ms: median(runs.map { |r| r["p99_ms"] }),
    rss_kb: rss_kb, rss_mb: rss_kb && rss_kb / 1024.0,
    pss_kb: pss_kb, pss_mb: pss_kb && pss_kb / 1024.0,
    # Per-GB against the FAIR number: an over-counted denominator makes
    # a multi-process lane look more efficient than it is... and a
    # single-process one look worse.
    rps_per_gb: (req && (pss_kb || rss_kb) && (pss_kb || rss_kb) > 0) ?
                 req / ((pss_kb || rss_kb) / 1024.0 / 1024.0) : nil }
end
File.write(sj, JSON.pretty_generate(cells))

md = +"# campfire: Rails vs the emitted tree\n\n"
md << "_#{records.size} measurements across #{cells.size} cells_\n\n"
cell = ->(v, p) { v.nil? ? "—" : ("%.#{p}f" % v) }
cells.group_by { |c| c[:endpoint] }.each do |ep, eg|
  md << "## `#{ep}`\n\n"
  md << "| lane | req/sec | p50 (ms) | p99 (ms) | RSS (MB) | req/sec/GB |\n"
  md << "|------|--------:|---------:|---------:|---------:|-----------:|\n"
  eg.sort_by { |c| c[:target] }.each do |c|
    md << "| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |\n" % [
      c[:target], cell.(c[:req_sec], 1), cell.(c[:p50_ms], 2),
      cell.(c[:p99_ms], 2), cell.(c[:rss_mb], 0), cell.(c[:rps_per_gb], 0) ]
  end
  # The ratio, stated rather than left to the reader — and only when both
  # operands were measured in this run.
  ruby = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == "ruby" }
  if ruby && ruby[:req_sec] && ruby[:req_sec] > 0
    %w[rails rails-nocache].each do |name|
      cmp = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == name }
      next unless cmp && cmp[:req_sec]
      md << "\n_%s / ruby = %.2fx_\n" % [name, cmp[:req_sec] / ruby[:req_sec]]
    end
  end
  if eg.any? { |c| c[:target] == "rails" } && eg.any? { |c| c[:target] == "rails-nocache" }
    md << "\n`rails` serves campfire's message fragments from Redis " \
          "(`cached: true`); `rails-nocache` renders them. The emitted " \
          "tree has no fragment cache, so `rails-nocache` is the lane " \
          "that compares renderers and `rails` is the lane that says " \
          "what a deployment costs.\n"
  end
  md << "\n"
end

if File.exist?(parity) && !File.read(parity).empty?
  md << "## What each lane rendered\n\n"
  md << "A throughput number for a page that is not the same page is not\n"
  md << "a comparison. One authenticated GET per lane per endpoint,\n"
  md << "before any load:\n\n"
  md << "| lane | endpoint | status | tags | bytes |\n|---|---|--:|--:|--:|\n"
  File.readlines(parity, chomp: true).each do |line|
    lane, ep, st, tags, bytes = line.split("\t")
    md << "| %s | `%s` | %s | %s | %s |\n" % [lane, ep, st.sub("status=", ""),
                                              tags.sub("tags=", ""), bytes.sub("bytes=", "")]
  end
  md << "\n"
end
File.write(sm, md)
RUBY

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publish the emitted SPINEL tree alongside the run. Campfire has no
# spinel lane — it does not compile yet — so nothing above produces this
# tree and nothing below measures it. It is packed for provenance: a
# dated, stamped record of what we emitted on a given day, so that when
# the tree does become useful to point an upstream issue at, the history
# is already there. It cannot be created retroactively.
#
# Strict on purpose (no --allow-unsupported): campfire's strict emit is
# at 0 errors and CI holds that ceiling, so a regression should be loud
# here rather than silently packed.
#
# The status recorded is what a CONSUMER sees — a plain `spinel -c`, no
# SP_COLLECT_ERRORS. The emit's own error count is the wrong number to
# stamp an archive with: it is 0 today while spinel still refuses the
# tree, so recording it would mark as clean something that does not
# build. Under SP_COLLECT_ERRORS spinel does write C, but the gap sites
# are recovered from, so that output is truncated units rather than a
# program -- also not a status worth recording.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
pack_spinel_tree() {
    # Already emitted (and built) above when a spinel lane ran; only
    # re-emit for a run that asked for no spinel lane at all.
    if (( WANT_SPINEL == 0 )); then
        step "transpiling campfire → spinel (archive only)"
        if ! cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target spinel "$APP" \
                -o "$SP_EMIT" > "$LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log" 2>&1; then
            fail "strict spinel emit failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log) — tree not packed"
            return 0
        fi
    fi

    local rh sp build_status
    rh="$(cd "$REPO_ROOT" && git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
    sp="$(command -v spinel >/dev/null 2>&1 && spinel --version 2>/dev/null || echo 'not on PATH')"

    # One plain front-end run. Non-fatal either way: this records the
    # state, it does not gate on it.
    if command -v spinel >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if (cd "$SP_EMIT" && spinel -c --rbs . main.rb -o /dev/null) \
                > "$LOG_DIR/spinel-front-end.log" 2>&1; then
            build_status="front end: OK (emitted C)"
        else
            # spinel prints warnings on the same `spinel: <file>:<line>:`
            # shape as errors, so a prefix match picks up the first
            # warning rather than the refusal. Filter on the marker.
            build_status="front end: REFUSED — $(grep -E '^spinel: ' "$LOG_DIR/spinel-front-end.log" | grep -v ': warning: ' | head -1 | sed 's/^spinel: //' | cut -c1-160)"
        fi
    else
        build_status="front end: not checked (spinel not on PATH)"
    fi

    # UNQUOTED heredoc — it interpolates $rh, $sp and $(date), which is
    # the point. That also makes every BACKTICK a command substitution:
    # prose written as `spinel` RUNS spinel, splices its stdout into the
    # shipped file and leaks its stderr to the operator's console. Three
    # of them shipped in the 2026-09-04 archive, which is how `wrk`'s
    # usage text ended up in the middle of a sentence. Escape every
    # backtick in this block: \` .
    cat > "$SP_EMIT/REPRODUCE.md" <<REPRO
# campfire — spinel tree, as emitted

Emitted by roundhouse \`$rh\` from once-campfire. Strict emit, no
\`--allow-unsupported\`.

    roundhouse : $rh
    spinel     : $sp
    packed     : $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    $build_status

## What this is

The tree the \`spinel\` lanes on the benchmark page were built from. It
compiles, links and serves: the same binary answers campfire's room
pages under \`wrk\` and holds thousands of WebSocket connections under
\`scripts/campfire-cable-sweep\`. Until 2026-09-04 this archive carried a
paragraph saying it did not compile — accurate when it was written, and
long outlived by the tree beside it.

The dated copy is still the point: when a wall here turns out to be
spinel's rather than ours, this is the tree to point the issue at, and it
will not have moved underneath the issue.

## Build it

    # needs spinel + spinel_rbs_extract on PATH
    spinel -c --rbs . main.rb -o out.c    # front end only
    make build                            # through to a binary

Binary media (images, sounds, test fixture files) is excluded — 61 MB of
campfire's emit, none of it read by the compiler. Re-emit from the
roundhouse revision above if you need the assets.

\`SP_COLLECT_ERRORS=1\` on the first command reports every gap in one run
rather than stopping at the first — useful while the count is still
falling, but note it recovers from each gap, so the C it writes is
truncated units and not a program.
REPRO

    # Binary media is 61 MB of campfire's emit and none of it is the
    # subject: a 46 MB test fixture (earth.png), a .mov, the sound
    # library. The spinel compile reads .rb and .rbs only, so the
    # archive carries the source tree and says it left the assets out.
    tar czf "$OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz" -C "$(dirname "$SP_EMIT")" \
        --exclude=build --exclude=bench-work --exclude=storage \
        --exclude='*.sqlite3' --exclude='*.o' --exclude='*.log' \
        --exclude=spin.lock --exclude=node_modules \
        --exclude='*.png' --exclude='*.jpg' --exclude='*.jpeg' \
        --exclude='*.gif' --exclude='*.webp' --exclude='*.ico' \
        --exclude='*.svg' --exclude='*.mp3' --exclude='*.mov' \
        --exclude='*.mp4' --exclude='*.woff' --exclude='*.woff2' \
        "$(basename "$SP_EMIT")" 2>/dev/null \
      && ok "packed spinel tree -> $OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz ($(du -h "$OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)) [$build_status]" \
      || fail "could not pack the spinel tree"
    rm -f "$SP_EMIT/REPRODUCE.md"
}
pack_spinel_tree

ok "results in $OUT_DIR"
echo
cat "$OUT_DIR/summary.md"
